+**Latest structure revision:** [Battlefield structure, Threat Lab and integration gates](gameplay/BATTLEFIELD_STRUCTURE.md). Building branch c720c13 passes standalone validation/reproduction but is not merged. Plan includes configurable Soldier/LSW forces, visible scanner, event-driven states, spatial/aircraft/art pipeline and local diagnostic logging. The creator confirmed a shoot-out shield dome with incoming-shot interception and unrestricted body passage; enemies can rush inside and fight normally. This remains a planning turn.
+
+**Latest class/content planning request:** [Soldier and LSW design + source audit](gameplay/CLASS_AND_CONTENT_PIPELINE.md), [ten gadgets and ten powers](gameplay/CONTENT_PROPOSALS.md), [seven actual local skills](gameplay/CONTENT_SKILLS.md), and [ordered implementation](superpowers/plans/2026-09-11-class-content-pipeline.md). This update is planning/documentation only. Only Soldiers receive conventional equipment/backpacks; non-soldier LSWs have up to two gadgets and no backpack; powered flyers cannot use guns. Enemy resistance intelligence is scanner-gated. Preserve SOL/VEGA. Location-aware ambient audio is deferred. These current rules supersede conflicting generic inventory/flight-injection examples below and in earlier drafts.
+
 **Keep track here:** [decision and delivery tracker](gameplay/TRACKER.md). Latest additions: preserve the liked desert, police escalating to military, rush combinations that end in recovery space, and an aimed spinning throw. [Clip and ESF evidence](reports/2026-09-11-melee-esf-reference-review.md) distinguishes observed motion, creator direction and proposed rules.
 
 **Execution authorized, September 11:** the creator approved the tracker and requested sustained implementation plus dream-loop visual improvement. Person pickup/carry during flight, Tab melee mode and zombies join the active scope. “Proposed” numerical tuning remains implementation judgment, not a request to repeat the whole design interview. Current delivery status stays in the tracker.
